In C programming, you can terminate a program using the return statement. The return statement is used to exit a function and return control to the caller. If the return statement is used in the main function, it will terminate the program.

Here is an example of how to use the return statement to terminate a program:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    printf("Hello, world!\n");
    return 0; // This will terminate the program
}
```

In this example, the return statement is used to exit the main function and terminate the program. The argument to the return statement, 0, is the return value of the program. In most cases, the return value of a program is not used by the operating system, so you can use any value you like.

Alternatively, you can use the exit function to terminate a program. The exit function takes an integer argument that is used as the return value of the program. Here is an example of how to use the exit function:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main() {
    printf("Hello, world!\n");
    exit(0); // This

